  "summary": "The medtech industry is navigating significant policy headwinds, with AdvaMed highlighting tariffs, FDA user fees, payment coverage, and cybersecurity as critical priorities for the sector. Meanwhile, deal activity continues briskly: Merck KGaA is acquiring Silence Therapeutics for $1.1 billion to gain an RNA therapy for polycythemia vera, Stryker is investing $400 million in CMR Surgical with an acquisition option, and several startups including XRlabs and Phantom GI are raising capital for AI surgical platforms and minimally invasive devices respectively. Clinical AI adoption is accelerating for treatment planning and triage applications—though the focus appears to be shifting away from diagnosis toward workflow optimization—while separate concerns over the No Surprises Act reveal that nonprofit arbitrators are accumulating substantial wealth administering the system. Political tensions around vaccines and reproductive health are shaping the midterm landscape, vaccine hesitancy is becoming a GOP liability among voters, and the Trump administration is creating a new NIH division focused on women's health, though details remain sparse.",
